//Move Assignment Operator

The move assignment operator transfers resources from one already-existing object to another already-existing object.

It uses operator= with an rvalue reference.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int* age;

    Student(int a) {
        age = new int(a);
    }

    Student& operator=(Student&& s) {
        delete age;
        age = s.age;
        s.age = nullptr;
        return *this;
    }
};

Now:

Student s1(20);
Student s2(25);

s2 = std::move(s1);

Here:

s1 already exists
s2 already exists
Resources of s1 are transferred to s2

After the move:

s1 → nullptr

s2 → [20]
Move Constructor vs Move Assignment

Move constructor:

Student s2 = std::move(s1);

→ Creates a new object.

Move assignment:

s2 = std::move(s1);

→ s2 already exists.

Simple definition:

Move assignment = transfers resources from one existing object to another existing object.